faciolock for Debian and Ubuntu
===============================

Installing this package does not enable face authentication. Nothing
authenticates by face until you install libpam-faciolock and run
pam-auth-update, and that is a separate, deliberate step with its own recovery
path -- see /usr/share/doc/libpam-faciolock/README.Debian.

Setting it up
-------------

The daemon is socket-activated: /run/faciolock.sock exists from boot, nothing
runs behind it, and the first connection starts faciolockd. It exits again
after 30 idle seconds, so an unused machine carries no resident face daemon.

  1. Find the camera and record which tier your hardware is:

       sudo faciolockctl diagnose --write

     This picks the IR node -- not the RGB one, and not the metadata nodes that
     sit beside it -- and writes /etc/faciolock/faciolockd.toml. The package
     deliberately ships no config file: diagnose refuses to overwrite one that
     already exists, so a placeholder would permanently break this command.

  2. Enrol. Three poses, in a room dark enough that the IR emitter is what is
     lighting your face:

       sudo faciolockctl enroll $USER

  3. Test it without touching any PAM file at all:

       sudo faciolockctl test $USER

  4. For anything that authenticates inside your own desktop session -- the KDE
     lock screen above all -- join the group the socket is gated on, then log
     out and back in:

       sudo gpasswd -a $USER faciolock-auth

     A package cannot do this for you: which humans may ask the daemon about
     themselves is an administrator's decision, not an installer's. Without it
     the lock screen falls through to the password field silently and forever.

What this will not do
---------------------

Face authentication is a convenience factor, not a password replacement. A
password stays reachable on every path, and every failure mode -- daemon down,
not enrolled, rate-limited, refused -- falls through to it.

Two limitations at this version are measured rather than suspected:

  * Daylight needs a usable emitter signal. Every frame pair must show the
    emitter over the ambient light (OFF/ON <= 0.9 and ON-OFF >= 5 levels); a
    weak, irregular or saturated capture refuses and you type your password.
    Recorded daylight captures pass with these defaults. A daylight run on
    hardware has not been observed at this version. The margin the earlier
    darkness threshold held against a pulsed infrared source went with the
    change, and extra infrared beside a genuinely enrolled face is not
    categorically rejected.

  * A printed photograph is not reliably rejected by the liveness checks. The
    published bar for this project is the same one Windows Hello sits at: a
    purpose-built artefact defeats it. If you need a second factor that resists
    a motivated attacker, use a hardware token.

Both are tracked in the bug log shipped with this package.

Removing it
-----------

Purging faciolock leaves your enrolment and its encryption key behind in
/var/lib/faciolock, on purpose -- a package that deletes biometric templates on
an upgrade gone sideways is a package that costs you a re-enrolment. Remove
them yourself when you mean it:

  sudo rm -rf /var/lib/faciolock /etc/faciolock

 -- Somesh Rao <cokasomeshrao@outlook.com>
